  "description": "Compute the standard error of the mean for multiple independent samples and calculate their overall average standard error.\n\nStandard error reflects the sampling variability of the estimator and scales inversely with the square root of sample size.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\n\\operatorname{SE}_i = \\frac{s_i}{\\sqrt{n_i}}, \\quad \\overline{\\operatorname{SE}} = \\frac{1}{K}\\sum_{i=1}^K \\operatorname{SE}_i\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Compute sample standard deviation using Bessel's correction (ddof=1) for each sample\n2. Calculate standard error of the mean for each group\n3. Compute arithmetic mean across all sample standard errors\n\n### Returns\nDictionary with 'standard_errors' list and 'average_se' scalar.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Standard Error Comparison\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nReal-world data is inherently noisy and uncertain. Probability theory and statistics provide principled tools to quantify that uncertainty, model how data is generated, and evaluate confidence in empirical results. **Standard Error Comparison** evaluates probability distributions or statistical tests to summarize sample characteristics.\n\nStandard error reflects the sampling variability of the estimator and scales inversely with the square root of sample size. In this problem, the goal is to: compute the standard error of the mean for multiple independent samples and calculate their overall average standard error.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{SE}_i = \\frac{s_i}{\\sqrt{n_i}}, \\quad \\overline{\\operatorname{SE}} = \\frac{1}{K}\\sum_{i=1}^K \\operatorname{SE}_i\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Compute sample standard deviation using Bessel's correction (ddof=1) for each sample\n2. **Phase 2**: Calculate standard error of the mean for each group\n3. **Phase 3**: Compute arithmetic mean across all sample standard errors\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Statistical Significance**: Machine learning A/B testing and model evaluation rely on p-values and confidence intervals to ensure observed improvements are not just random noise.\n* **Tail Probabilities & Underflow**: In deep generative models or Bayesian inference, computing log-probabilities prevents underflow when dealing with very small probability values in distribution tails.\n* **Moments & Distributions**: Examining mean, variance, skewness, and kurtosis helps data practitioners detect outliers and choose appropriate normalization strategies.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: samples = [[10.0, 12.0, 14.0, 11.0, 13.0], [20.0, 25.0, 22.0, 23.0]]\n\nOutput: {\"standard_errors\": [0.707107, 1.040833], \"average_se\": 0.87397}\n\nExplanation: Evaluated probstat_standard_errors under standard test conditions."
  ],
